}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In order to show that the ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th-powers of the points are ",
  StyleBox["independent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", it is sufficient to find an ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th-degree polynomial that ",
  StyleBox["vanishes",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at all points of the array ",
  StyleBox["except at one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (Why?)  Now, to each line there of course corresponds a ",
  StyleBox["linear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["xyz-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "polynomial which vanishes at points on the line and is ",
  StyleBox["non-zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " everywhere else.  The product  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  of these linear polynomials corresponding to the  ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  lines of a covering which leaves a point  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  exposed,  is a polynomial of degree  ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  which vanishes at every point of the array ",
  StyleBox["except at",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The argument about independence of ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-th powers would apply to ",
  StyleBox["any",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " configuration with the property used in the above proof.  Since the \
dimension of the space of ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th degree homogeneous polynomials is known, we can derive an immediate \
corollary."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " A configuration of points in the plane arranged so that removing one \
point always allows the remainder to be covered with ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " lines \[LongDash] none passing through the removed point \[LongDash] has \
cardinality at most ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(1\/2\ \((n + 1)\)\ \((n + 2)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell["Here is another corollary.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " An ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th-degree polynomial vanishing at all points in such a configuration must \
be ",
  StyleBox["identically zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Otherwise said, there is no non-trivial ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th-degree curve passing through all of the points."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "This is still not a very constructive proof, and it would be interesting \
to have ",
  StyleBox["explicit formulae",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to show how every ",
  StyleBox["monomial",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is a sum of ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th powers.  (Petkovesek found one such algorithm.)  One thing that makes \
this problem somewhat messy is that there is no ",
  StyleBox["unique",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " basis of powers and no ",
  StyleBox["unique",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " representation.  And what about representing a polynomial as a ",
  StyleBox["minimal number",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th powers?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell["\<\
Here is an application to finding a general formula that can be \
derived using powers.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(n\  \[GreaterEqual] \ m\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a linear ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomial, and if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is any ",
  StyleBox["m-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "degree ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomial, then:\n",
  StyleBox["\t\t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{\(PD[f\^n, h]\), "==", 
          RowBox[{
            FormBox[
              RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
                    {
                      StyleBox["n",
                        FontSlant->"Plain"]},
                    {
                      StyleBox["m",
                        FontSlant->"Plain"]}
                    }], ")"}],
              "TraditionalForm"], " ", \(PD[f\^m, h]\), " ", 
            \(f\^\(n - m\)\)}]}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".\t\t\t\t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Inasmuch as both sides of the equation are ",
  StyleBox["linear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", we only have to prove this for ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th ",
  StyleBox["powers of of linear polynomials",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (Why?)  Note also that by previous theorems ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f\^m, h]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "is the ",
  StyleBox["evaluation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " at the coefficients of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now in case ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(h == g\^m\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", where ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is linear, then by a previous theorem we have:\n",
  StyleBox["\t\t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f\^n, g\^m] == 
        \(\(n!\)\ PD[f, g]\^m\ f\^\(n - m\)\)\/\(\((n - m)\)!\)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\t\t\t\t\n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "But when ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(m == n\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", we have:\n",
  StyleBox["\t\t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f\^m, g\^m] == \(m!\)\ PD[f, g]\^m\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\t\t\t\t\n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "We thus derive:\n",
  StyleBox["\t\t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f\^n, g\^m] == 
        \(\(n!\)\ PD[f\^m, g\^m]\ f\^\(n - m\)\)\/\(\(\((n - m)\)!\)\ 
            \(m!\)\)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\t\t\t\t\n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "This establishes what we wanted for ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th powers.",
  StyleBox["  Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Invariance of polydifferentiation", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can consider our study as involving the vector space ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^n\)[x, y, z]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " of ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-degree homogeneous ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials on the one hand (with complex coefficients in ",
  StyleBox["\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]", "InlineInput"],
  "), the vector space  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^m\)[u, v, w]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " of ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-degree homogeneous ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials on the other hand.  These vector spaces are closed under ",
  StyleBox["addition",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["scalar multiplication",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  The operation of ",
  StyleBox["multiplication",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " can be considered as an operation which ",
  StyleBox["adds",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "degrees:\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(\[CenterDot]\( : \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^n\)[x, y, z]\[Times]\ 
            \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^m\)[x, y, z]\[LongRightArrow]\ 
              \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^\(n + m\)\)[x, y, z]\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(\[CenterDot]\( : \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^n\)[u, v, w]\[Times]\ 
            \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^m\)[u, v, w]\[LongRightArrow]\ 
              \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^\(n + m\)\)[u, v, w]\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Warning.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Do not forget that to make sense of these remarks the exceptional ",
  StyleBox["0", "InlineInput"],
  " polynomial has to be considered here as having ",
  StyleBox["every",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " degree."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Polydifferentiation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", however, ",
  StyleBox["subtracts",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " degrees.  In case ",
  StyleBox["m \[LessEqual] n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", we have:\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"PD", ":", 
          RowBox[{
          \(\(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^n\)[x, y, z]\), "\[Times]", " ", 
            RowBox[{
            \(\(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^m\)[u, v, w]\), "\[LongRightArrow]", 
              " ", 
              RowBox[{
                SuperscriptBox["\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]", 
                  RowBox[{"n", 
                    StyleBox["-",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], "m"}]], "[", \(x, y, z\), 
                "]"}]}]}]}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nBut, in case ",
  StyleBox["m \[GreaterEqual] n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", we have:\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"PD", ":", 
          RowBox[{
          \(\(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^n\)[x, y, z]\), "\[Times]", " ", 
            RowBox[{
            \(\(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^m\)[u, v, w]\), "\[LongRightArrow]", 
              " ", 
              RowBox[{
                SuperscriptBox["\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]", 
                  StyleBox[\(m - n\),
                    FontWeight->"Bold"]], "[", \(u, v, w\), "]"}]}]}]}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nAnd then, if  ",
  StyleBox["m == n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", we have:\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(PD : \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^n\)[x, y, z]\[Times]\ 
            \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^n\)[u, v, w]\[LongRightArrow]\ 
              \[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nAnd this last case is how we consider having ",
  StyleBox["dual vector spaces",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "When ",
  StyleBox["M", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is a non-singular matrix, then the corresponding linear transformations \
map polynomials in this way:\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(TP[M] : 
          \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^n\)[u, v, w]\[LongRightArrow]\ 
            \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^n\)[u, v, w]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(TL[M] : 
          \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^n\)[x, y, z]\[LongRightArrow]\ 
            \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^n\)[x, y, z]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?TP\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \("Global`TP"\)], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
    InterpretationBox[
      StyleBox[
        \(TP[m_]\  := \ 
          Tidy[#1\  /. \ Thread[{u, \ v, \ w}\  -> \ m\  . \ {u, \ v, \ w}]]
              \ &\ \),
        ShowStringCharacters->True,
        NumberMarks->True],
      InputForm[ 
        Definition[ TP]],
      Editable->True,
      AutoDelete->True]], "Print"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Warning.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "In each case above we have a non-singular linear transformation of the \
indicated space, but keep in mind that ",
  StyleBox["not all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " linear transformations are of the form ",
  StyleBox["TP[M]", "InlineInput"],
  " or ",
  StyleBox["TL[M]", "InlineInput"],
  " when we consider degrees ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(n > 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (Why?)  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The question of whether two higher-degree polynomials are ",
  StyleBox["projectively equivalent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " by a ",
  StyleBox["TP[M]", "InlineInput"],
  " or ",
  StyleBox["TL[M]", "InlineInput"],
  " is an interesting and sometimes difficult question."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 0.4, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "However, the projective transformations as represented by matrices ",
  StyleBox["do",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " preserve polydifferentiation.  We can say just as projective \
transformations are ",
  StyleBox["algebra automorphisms",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", they are also ",
  StyleBox["differentiation automorphisms",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Specifically, we have:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  ",
  StyleBox["(",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["i",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[")  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "If ",
  StyleBox["M", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is a non-singular matrix, ",
  StyleBox["m \[LessEqual] n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["f", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is an",
  StyleBox[" n-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "degree ",
  StyleBox["xyz-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "polynomial, and ",
  StyleBox["g", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is an",
  StyleBox[" m-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "degree ",
  StyleBox["uvw-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "polynomial, then we have:\n\t\t",
  StyleBox["TL[M][PD[f, g]] == PD[TL[M][f], TP[M][g]]",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\n",
  StyleBox["(",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["ii",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[")  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "In case ",
  StyleBox["m \[GreaterEqual] n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", there is a ",
  StyleBox["dual result",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with ",
  StyleBox["TP[M]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "on the left",
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  \n",
  StyleBox["(",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["iii",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[")  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "In case ",
  StyleBox["m == n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", then there is ",
  StyleBox["no",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " transformation on the left, as scalars are always ",
  StyleBox["left fixed",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "As we already know, the result is true for ",
  StyleBox["linear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (= first-degree) polynomials by the definition of the transformations \
based on the fact that the two linear spaces of linear polynomials are ",
  StyleBox["dual",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to each other under ",
  StyleBox["PD",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " as dot product. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, projective transformations are defined on ",
  StyleBox["higher-degree",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " polynomials by ",
  StyleBox["substitution",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and they are therefore automorphisms of the ring of polynomials.  \
Because, the desired formula in the theorem is ",
  StyleBox["bilinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["g",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", we need only prove it for ",
  StyleBox["powers of linear polynomials",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  In this case it follows from the linear result by invoking a formula \
proved before:\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\(TL[M]\)[PD[l\^n, p\^m]]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\n\t\t== ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\(n!\)\/\(\((n - m)\)!\)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[l, p]\^m\ \((\(TL[M]\)[l])\)\^\(n - m\)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" \n\t\t== ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\(n!\)\/\(\((n - m)\)!\)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[\(TL[M]\)[l], \(TP[M]\)[p]]\^m\ \(TL[M]\)[l]\^\(n - m\)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\n\t\t== ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[\(TL[M]\)[l]\^n, \(TP[M]\)[p]\^m]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A consequence of this important result is the observation that any \
(homogeneous) relationships we define by combining algebra with \
polydifferentiation ",
  StyleBox["must",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " have ",
  StyleBox["geometric significance",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", because the relationship must be ",
  StyleBox["preserved by all projective transformations",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  This is a very considerable ",
  StyleBox["generalization",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the principle that projective transformations preserve the ",
  StyleBox["incidence",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of points and lines."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 0.4, 0]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Polynomial independence", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Independence notions for points", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  A sequence of ",
  StyleBox["k",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " points is said to be ",
  StyleBox["n-independent",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " if, and only if, the space of ",
  StyleBox["n-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "degree curves passing through them has ",
  StyleBox["projective dimension",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(1\/2\ n\ \((n + 3)\) - k\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The above is the ",
  StyleBox["geometric",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " definition of independence, but we can translate the condition into an ",
  StyleBox["algebraic test",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at once:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proposition",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Points",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(p\_1\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[", ... , ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(p\_k\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are ",
  StyleBox["n-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "independent if, and only if, the ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th powers ",
  StyleBox[" \n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\%n\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ... , ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_k\%n\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  are linearly independent as ",
  StyleBox["uvw-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "polynomials."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell["Here are some results about indepencence.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "If ",
  StyleBox["k \[LessEqual] n+1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", then any sequence of ",
  StyleBox["k",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["distinct points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["n-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "independent."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " We argue by ",
  StyleBox["induction",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  The basis case of ",
  StyleBox["n == 1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is obvious.  "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now assume the result for ",
  StyleBox["n-1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and argue for ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Suppose the ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th powers  ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\%n\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ... , ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_k\%n\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are linearly ",
  StyleBox["dependent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  This means that we have a relationship:\n \t\t ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\(\(\(a\_1\) p\_1\%n\  + \)\ \)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ...  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\(+\ \(a\ \)\_k\) p\_k\%n\  == \ 0\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\nwith not all coefficients equal to ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Indeed, at least ",
  StyleBox["two",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the coefficients must be non-zero.  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["m",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be a ",
  StyleBox["line",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " passing through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_k\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " passing through any of the other points.  By differentiation of the above \
equation by the linear form ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["m",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", we cancel the last term and find:\n        ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\(\(\(a\_1\) n\ PD[m, \ p\_1] p\_1\%\(n - 1\)\  + \)\ \)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["... ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
      \(\(+\ \(a\ \)\_\(k - 1\)\) n\ PD[m, \ p\_\(k - 1\)] 
            p\_\(k - 1\)\%\(n - 1\)\  == \ 0\), 
        StyleBox[",",
          FontWeight->"Plain"]}]],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Since at least ",
  StyleBox["one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the coefficients in this equation is non zero, this means that the ",
  StyleBox["shorter sequence",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is not ",
  StyleBox["(n-1)-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "independent, which is a ",
  StyleBox["contradiction",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(k\  \[LessEqual] \ 2\ n + 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then any sequence of ",
  StyleBox["k",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " distinct points, ",
  StyleBox["no three of which are collinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", is ",
  StyleBox["n-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "independent."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "We argue again by induction on ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  The case of ",
  StyleBox["n == 1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is obvious.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now assume the result for ",
  StyleBox["n-1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and argue for ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Suppose the ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th powers  ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\%n\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ... , ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(p\_k\%n\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are linearly ",
  StyleBox["dependent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  This means that we have a relationship:\n \t\t ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\(\(\(a\_1\) p\_1\%n\  + \)\ \)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ...  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{\(\(+\ \(a\ \)\_k\) p\_k\%n\  == \ 0\), 
        StyleBox[",",
          FontWeight->"Plain"]}]],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\nwith not all coefficients equal to ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Indeed, by the previous theorem, at least ",
  StyleBox["(n+2)",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " of the coefficients must be non-zero.  Since we are considering a case \
where ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(n \[GreaterEqual] 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", this means that at least ",
  StyleBox["three ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "coefficents are non zero.  If we let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["m",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the line passing through",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(p\_k\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(p\_\(k - 1\)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", then\[LongDash]by assumption\[LongDash]it ",
  StyleBox["does not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " pass through any of the other points.  By differentiation of the equation \
by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["m",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", we cancel the last two terms and find:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "      ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\(\(\(a\_1\) n\ PD[m, \ p\_1] p\_1\%\(n - 1\)\  + \)\ \)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["... ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
      \(\(+\ \(a\ \)\_\(k - 2\)\) n\ PD[m, \ p\_\(k - 2\)] 
            p\_\(k - 2\)\%\(n - 1\)\  == \ 0\), 
        StyleBox[",",
          FontWeight->"Plain"]}]],
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This means that the shorter sequence is not ",
  StyleBox["(n-1)-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "independent and is of length at most ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(2\ n - 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", which is a ",
  StyleBox["contradiction",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "If  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      FormBox[
        StyleBox[\(k \[LessEqual] \(5\ n\)\/2 + 1\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"],
        "TextForm"]]],
  " , then any sequence of ",
  StyleBox["k",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " distinct points, no ",
  StyleBox["three",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of which are ",
  StyleBox["collinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and no ",
  StyleBox["six",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are ",
  StyleBox["conconic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", is ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-independent."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "We argue again by induction on ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  The cases ",
  StyleBox["n == 1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["n == 2 ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "are obvious.  Now assume the result for ",
  StyleBox["n-2",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and argue for ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Suppose the ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th powers  ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\%n\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ... , ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_k\%n\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are linearly ",
  StyleBox["dependent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  This means that we have a relationship:\n \t\t ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\(\(\(a\_1\) p\_1\%n\  + \)\ \)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ...  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{\(\(+\ \(a\ \)\_k\) p\_k\%n\  == \ 0\), 
        StyleBox[",",
          FontWeight->"Plain"]}]],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\nwith not all coefficients equal to ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Indeed, by the previous theorem, at least ",
  StyleBox["(2n+2)",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " of the coefficients must be non-zero.  Thus, ",
  StyleBox["k",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " has to be larger than ",
  StyleBox["8",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Take ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to be any non-zero conic passing through the last five points of the \
sequence, ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(p\_\(k - 4\)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[", ... , ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(p\_k\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " .  By dimensions of the dual space of the space of quadratic ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials, such an ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " must ",
  StyleBox["exist",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  By ",
  StyleBox["assumption",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", it does not pass through any of the other points.  By ",
  StyleBox["differentiation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the equation by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", we find (after dropping a common numerical factor):"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "      ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\(\(a\_1\ PD[f, \ p\_1\^2] p\_1\%\(n - 2\)\  + \)\ \)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["...  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
      \(\(+\ \(a\ \)\_\(k - 5\)\)\ PD[f, \ p\_\(k - 5\)\^2] 
            p\_\(k - 5\)\%\(n - 2\)\  == \ 0\), 
        StyleBox[",",
          FontWeight->"Plain"]}]],
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This means that the shorter sequence of length at most ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(5\ n\)\/2 + 1 - 5 == \(5\ \((n - 2)\)\)\/2 + 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is not ",
  StyleBox["(n-2)",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-independent, which is a contradiction.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Are these the best results?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Curves through given points", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
Here is an important immediate corollary of the proofs of \
independence.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Given ",
  StyleBox["five",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points, no three collinear, there is exactly one ",
  StyleBox["conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " passing through them."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " Given such five points, how do you ",
  StyleBox["find",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the conic passing through them (as a polynomial)?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We will answer this question later when we study conics more.  Our \
independence results do not have as sharp a consequences for ",
  StyleBox["cubics",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as we derived for conics, however."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Any sequence of ",
  StyleBox["8",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " distinct points\[LongDash]no three of which are collinear and no six \
conconic\[LongDash]determines a unique ",
  StyleBox["pencil of cubics",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As we shall see, there are configurations of ",
  StyleBox["9",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " points\[LongDash]no three of which are collinear and no six conconic\
\[LongDash]which are ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-independent.  The reason for the trouble here is that two ",
  StyleBox["distinct",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " cubics generally intersect in ",
  StyleBox["9",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " points."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Problem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " How should these theorems be generalized to higher degrees?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Defining Contact, Multiplicity, and Tangency", "Section",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Degree of contact and multiplicity", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Degree of contact", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So far we have been using very ",
  StyleBox["algebraic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " definitions, and we have to check in many examples that they agree with ",
  
  StyleBox["geometric intuition",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Suppose, for example, that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a homogeneous ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomial of degree ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " representing a ",
  StyleBox["curve",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a linear form representing a ",
  StyleBox["point",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Suppose further that the point lies on the curve:  \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, p\^n] == 0\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".",
  StyleBox["\n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Consider a line through the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a point on the line distinct from ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then the points \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]around\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " can be represented as linear combinations ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p + t\ q\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "   The question is to determine what ",
  StyleBox["degree of contact",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  the line has with the curve (aside from cutting the curve at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ").   Here is the algebraic definition we are going to use."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Let the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " lie on a curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and let the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be another point on a line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".   The line",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " has ",
  StyleBox["degree of contact",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " at ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "if, and only if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(t == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is an ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-fold root of the equation:\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
            RowBox[{"f", ",", 
              SuperscriptBox[
                RowBox[{"(", 
                  RowBox[{"p", "+", 
                    RowBox[{
                      StyleBox["t",
                        FontWeight->"Bold"], " ", "q"}]}], ")"}], "n"]}], 
            "]"}], "==", "0"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Clearly ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(t == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["is",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a root of this equation, because ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["on",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  If the line \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]cleanly\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] cuts \
the curve, then",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is but a ",
  StyleBox["simple root",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  If the line has ",
  StyleBox["closer contact",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with the curve, then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(t == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a ",
  StyleBox["multiple",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " root.  The exact multiplicity of this root in this equation is taken as \
the ",
  StyleBox["definition",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of degree of contact.  We have to investigate, however, how well this \
definition works out.  The first result shows the answer only depends on the \
",
  StyleBox["line",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and not on the choice of the extra point on the line."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The definition of degree of contact does not depend on the ",
  StyleBox["choice",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as the other point on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  We illustrate the argument with curves of degree ",
  StyleBox["6 ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "by doing some symbolic computation.  First we ",
  StyleBox["declare",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to be such a curve and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to represent points."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, p, q, Subscript]; \nf /: DegP[f] = 6; \n\(p /: DegP[p] = 1; 
    \)\), 
    \(\(q /: DegP[q] = 1; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Next we look at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
          RowBox[{"f", ",", 
            SuperscriptBox[
              RowBox[{"(", 
                RowBox[{"p", "+", 
                  RowBox[{
                    StyleBox["t",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], " ", "q"}]}], ")"}], "6"]}], 
          "]"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", which we can do by applying the well known ",
  StyleBox["Binomial Theorem",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(g\_1 = Expand[\((p + t\ q)\)\^6]\n\), 
    \(\((Collect[#1, t]&)\)[PD[f, g\_1]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(t == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "to be a ",
  StyleBox["triple",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "root of the equation ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, \((p + t\ q)\)\^6] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", we see that the first ",
  StyleBox["three terms",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " must vanish.  Let us ",
  StyleBox["declare",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " that this is so:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f /: PD[f, p\^6] = 0; \)\), 
    \(\(f /: PD[f, p\^5\ q] = 0; \)\), 
    \(\(f /: PD[f, p\^4\ q\^2] = 0; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Carrying out the computation of  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, \((p + t\ q)\)\^6]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " again, we see they are gone, allowing us to factor the result:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((Collect[#1, t]&)\)[PD[f, g\_1]] // Factor\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, we replace ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " by another point on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " \[Dash] ",
  StyleBox["different",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " from ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", namely ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ p + q\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " \[Dash] and do the computation again:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(g\_2 = Expand[\((p + t\ \((a\ p + q)\))\)\^6]; \n
    \((Collect[#1, t]&)\)[PD[f, g\_2]]\n\), 
    \(% // Factor\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We find that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(t\^3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " has the ",
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " coefficient as before.  So if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(t == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is ",
  StyleBox["exactly",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a triple root of the first equation based on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then it is also exactly a triple root of the second equation based on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ p\  + \ q\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is an example with a ",
  StyleBox["7",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th-degree polynomial."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, p, q, Subscript]; \n
    f = y\^2\ z\^5 - x\^7 - x\^6\ z - x\^4\ z\^3; \n\(p = w; \)\), 
    \(\(q = u; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[PD[f, \((p + t\ q)\)\^7]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The degree of contact with the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis (= ",
  StyleBox["PE[p,q]",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ") is ",
  StyleBox["4",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and here is a way of arranging the ",
  StyleBox["test",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for this outcome:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@{p\^7, p\^6\ q, p\^5\ q\^2, p\^4\ q\^3, p\^3\ q\^4}
        \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And here we change the choice of ",
  StyleBox["q",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(q = p\/5 + q; 
    \((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@{p\^7, p\^6\ q, p\^5\ q\^2, p\^4\ q\^3, p\^3\ q\^4}\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And we see that the test gives the",
  StyleBox[" same answer",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for a degree of contact.  In general, we can say:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Let the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " lie on a curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and let the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be ",
  StyleBox["any other",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " point on a line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".   The line",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " has ",
  StyleBox["degree of contact",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " at ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "if, and only if, \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["f",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[",",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              RowBox[{\(p\^\(n - i\)\), " ", 
                StyleBox[\(q\^i\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}], "]"}], "==", "0"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " whenever ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(0 \[LessEqual] i < m\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\^\(n - m\)\ q\^m] \[NotEqual] 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Multiplicity", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Two different lines through a point on a curve can have different degrees \
of contact with the curve.  It can even happen that all lines through the \
point have higher degree of contact than ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["1",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  However, the degree of contact cannot be higher than the degree of the \
curve itself.  (Why?)  We can make this observation into a definition."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A point on a curve is a ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["-tuple point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " if, and only if, ",
  StyleBox["every",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " line through the point has degree of contact ",
  StyleBox["at least",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and one line has degree of contact ",
  StyleBox["exactly",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is a ",
  StyleBox["test",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for determining the multiplicity of a point on a curve."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is a curve of degree ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["0 < m \[LessEqual] n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", then a point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is an ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["-tuple point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " if, and only if, \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\^\(n - m + 1\)] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[",   ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "but   ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\^\(n - m\)] \[NotEqual] 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  We will work this out",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "for the ",
  StyleBox["general",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["6",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th-degree curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Suppose ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a ",
  StyleBox["triple",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " point on this curve.  This means that ",
  StyleBox["every line",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " has ",
  StyleBox["degree of contact ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Consider a line passing through a an arbitrary point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "   As in the previous theorem, we may conclude that, for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(t == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to be a triple root of the equation \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
            RowBox[{"f", ",", 
              SuperscriptBox[
                RowBox[{"(", 
                  RowBox[{"p", "+", 
                    RowBox[{
                      StyleBox["t",
                        FontWeight->"Bold"], " ", "q"}]}], ")"}], "6"]}], 
            "]"}], "==", "0"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",\nit is necessary and sufficient that \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, p\^6] == PD[f, p\^5\ q] == PD[f, p\^4\ q\^2] == 0\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".\nNow we already know that the first quantity vanishes because ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["on",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the curve.  So there is nothing new here.  But to have ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, p\^5\ q]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " vanish for all points  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " means that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[PD[f, p\^5], q]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " vanishes ",
  StyleBox["for all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  By the dual pairing property, this means that the linear ",
  StyleBox["xyz-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "polynomial ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\^5]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " must vanish itself."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Next, we see that the quadratic polynomial ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\^4]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " has to vanish on all ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(q\^2\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  By the Generation Theorem, this means that it must also be the zero \
polynomial."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Finally, since ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(t == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a quadruple root for some choice of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "it follows that the cubic polynomial ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, p\^3]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " cannot be identically zero.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["Let us consider an example used previously.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f, p]; \)\), 
    \(\(f = y\^2\ z\^5 - x\^7 - x\^6\ z - x\^4\ z\^3; \)\), 
    \(\(p = w; \)\), 
    \(\((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@{p\^7, p\^6, p\^5, p\^4, p\^3}\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We have just shown that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a ",
  StyleBox["double",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "point on this curve.  Now we modify the curve a little."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, p]; p = w; \), 
    \(f = y\^3\ z\^4 - x\^7 - x\^6\ z; \n
    \((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@{p\^7, p\^6, p\^5, p\^4, p\^3}\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For the ",
  StyleBox["new",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " curve, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a ",
  StyleBox["triple ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "point.  And we try another variation:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, p]; p = w; \), 
    \(f = y\^3\ z\^4 - x\^7 + x\ y\^2\ z\^4; \n
    \((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@{p\^7, p\^6, p\^5, p\^4, p\^3}\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Again a ",
  StyleBox["triple",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " point.  We can graph the ",
  StyleBox["real points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the last curve by setting ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z == 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and then by using ",
  StyleBox["ImplicitPlot",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[y\^3 + x\ y\^2 == x\^7, {x, \(-2\), 2}, 
      Axes \[Rule] False]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This shows why the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is ",
  StyleBox["triple",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " very clearly.  We can also check the degree of contact of the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis and the ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis with the curve."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(q = u + w; r = v + w; \n
    \(PD[f, #]&\)/@{p\^7, \(p\^6\) q, \(p\^5\) q\^2, \(p\^4\) q\^3, 
        \(p\^3\) q\^4, p\^2\ q\^5, p\ q\^6, q\^7}\n\), 
    \(\(PD[f, #]&\)/@{p\^7, \(p\^6\) r, \(p\^5\) r\^2, \(p\^4\) r\^3, 
        \(p\^3\) r\^4, p\^2\ r\^5, p\ r\^6, r\^7}\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So!  The ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis has a very high degree of contact witht he curve, but the ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis has only degree of contact equal to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["3",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Points of tangency and inflection", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Tangency", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The example of the ",
  StyleBox["7",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th-degree curve we have just seen, suggests this definition:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "If a line at a ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-tuple point of a curve has degree of contact ",
  StyleBox["greater than",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", then it is said to be ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  at that point. "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 1.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Here is an illustration of a",
  StyleBox[" tangent to a cubic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f, p]; \)\), 
    \(\(f = x\^3 - y\^2\ z; \)\), 
    \(\(p = u + v + w; \)\), 
    \(\((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@{p\^3, p\^2, p, 1}\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "First, we see that the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is on the curve, and it is a ",
  StyleBox["simple point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Here is another point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and the parameterization of the points on the line they determine."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(q = \(-v\) + 2\ w; \)\), 
    \(Collect[PD[f, \((p + t\ q)\)\^3], {t}]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The degree of contact of the line with the curve is ",
  StyleBox["2",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  So the line joining ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["q",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is a ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Having two points on it, we can calculate it this way:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["PE[p,q]", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We see that this form for the tangent represents the ",
  StyleBox["same line",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\^2]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".   Here is a picture of the real part of the cubic and its tangent."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{y\^2 == x\^3, 3\ x == 2\ y + 1}, {x, \(-2\), 2}]; 
    \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox[" Question.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["Is there a theorem here about finding tangents?",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 2.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A second illustration concerns a ",
  StyleBox["double point of a cubic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f, p, q]; \)\), 
    \(\(f = x\^3 + y\^2\ z + x\ y\ z; \)\), 
    \(PD[f, w\^2]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  is a ",
  StyleBox["double point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  We can draw a picture."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[x\^3 + y\^2 + x\ y == 0, {x, \(-1\), 1}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["Now, look at the first derivative:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[PD[f, w]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now the two lines only intersect the cubic at the unique point ",
  StyleBox["w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  This means they have degree of contact ",
  StyleBox["greater",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["than",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["2",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Thus ",
  StyleBox["both",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " lines are ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the curve.  Here is a calculation that also shows multiple roots."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Solve[{f == 0, y == 0, z == 1}, {x, y, z}]\), 
    \(Solve[{f == 0, y == 0, z == 0}, {x, y, z}]\), 
    \(Solve[{f == 0, x + y == 0, z == 1}, {x, y, z}]\), 
    \(Solve[{f == 0, x + y == 0, z == 0}, {x, y, z}]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
And here is a picture of the situation of the two tangent \
lines.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{x\^3 + y\^2 + x\ y == 0, y == 0, x + y == 0}, {x, 
        \(-1\), 1}, Axes \[Rule] False, 
      PlotStyle \[Rule] {Red, Green, Blue}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox[" Question.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["Is there a theorem here about finding tangents?",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Inflection points", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  A simple point (",
  StyleBox["1-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "tuple point)  on a curve is an ",
  StyleBox["inflection point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "if the tangent to the curve at the point has degree of contact ",
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " or more."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " A point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is an inflection point on a curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " of degree ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " if, and only if, we have these ",
  StyleBox["three",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " conditions satisfied: \n\t",
  StyleBox["(",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["i",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[")  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, p\^n] == 0\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", \n\t",
  StyleBox["(",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["ii",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[")  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\^\(n - 1\)] \[NotEqual] 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", \n\t",
  StyleBox["(",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["iii",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[")  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, p\^\(n - 2\)]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "factors into linear factors."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Let  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  be an inflection point of  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " a point on the tangent of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Since degree of contact of the tangent at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is at least ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["3",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", we have ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, p\^\(n - 2\)\ q\^2] == 0\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "But this means that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " lies on the ",
  StyleBox["conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, p\^\(n - 2\)]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  As ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " was ",
  StyleBox["any point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the tangent, this conic contains the ",
  StyleBox["entire",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " tangent, and so the conic ",
  StyleBox["factors",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Conversely, assume that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\^\(n - 2\)]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "factors.   As ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, p\^\(n - 1\)] \[NotEqual] 0\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "by assumption, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a double point of this conic.  If the factors were equal,  ",
  StyleBox["all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points would be double; so there must be ",
  StyleBox["two distinct",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " factors.  "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the ",
  StyleBox["intersection",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the corresponding lines. It is a double point of the conic, so ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\^\(n - 2\)\ q] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ". Differentiating this equation with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", respectively, we get \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, p\^\(n - 1\)\ q] == PD[f, p\^\(n - 2\)\ q\^2] == 0\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[". ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " \nBy assumption,  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\^n] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "as well, and so the line ",
  StyleBox["PE[p,q]",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " has degree of contact at least ",
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is an example about ",
  StyleBox["cubics",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (since a conic cannot have inflection points).  The cubic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(x\^3\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["-",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[" ",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{"y", " ", 
            SuperscriptBox[
              StyleBox["z",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"]}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  " has ",
  StyleBox["only",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " inflection point.  The point is  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[x\^3 - y\ z\^2, #]&)\)/@{w\^3, w\^2, w}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The line represented by ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is the ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  And we see the ",
  StyleBox["degree of contact",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the line with the curve is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["3",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[x\^3 - y\ z\^2, #]&)\)/@{w\^3, w\^2\ u, w\ u\^2, u\^3}\)], "Input"],

Cell["The (real part of the) graph is well known:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[x\^3 == y, {x, \(-1\), 1}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But note that it is not especially clear from the picture that the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis actually ",
  StyleBox["has",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " higher degree contact with the curve than an ordinary tangent at some \
other point.  You have to do your ",
  StyleBox["algebra",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to make sure!"
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
},
FrontEndVersion->"X 3.0",
ScreenRectangle->{{0, 1280}, {0, 1024}},
ScreenStyleEnvironment->"Working",
WindowSize->{785, 556},
WindowMargins->{{195, Automatic}, {188, Automatic}},
Magnification->1,
StyleDefinitions -> "Classroom.nb"
]


